  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to construct periodic candidate functions based on domainspecific knowledge and mathematical optimization. Unlike the parent approach's reliance on continuous optimization, we will generate candidate solutions through discrete-toothed variations of symmetric fundamental patterns. Our rationale is that the optimal solution likely involves cyclic symmetry due to the problem's translational invariance characteristic. This approach reduces the search space by construction and leverages mathematical continuity between symmetries.\n\nThe method deliberately features step function constructions with discrete activation patterns derived from binary error-correcting codes. By imposing the integral constraint explicitly through a dimensional projection rather than iterative clipping, we maintain both feasibility and optimize the correlation structure in the time domain.\n\nImportant limitations include:\n- Strict enforcement of periodic structure may limit organic shape optimization\n- Sampling primarily takes place in discrete topological spaces\n- No adaptive smoothing mechanism to escape local optima\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nA candidate function h(x) is defined over the real line as:\nh_p(x) = (sum_{k=-\u221e}^{\u221e} (-1)^k \u03b4(x - k*P - \u03c6_k))_+ \n\nWhere \u03c6_k are phase offsets, and P is the fundamental period. The function is cut to [0,2] and discretized with n_points samples. To ensure sum(h) = 1:\nProject the function using the mapping: \nh_sampler(x) = integral_0^x h(x) dx normalized by the full integral over [0,2]\n\nThe critical derivation requires transforming between the domain and range spaces while:\n- Preserving both 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and integrality constraints\n- Maintaining explicit denominator constraints\n- Avoiding circular correlation approximations\n\nThe objective is exactly: max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx computed via direct linear correlation:\n\nL = max_{t\u2208[-2,2]} \u222b_{0}^{2-t} h(x)(1-h(x+t)) dx\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nThe search space is parameterized by (period P, amplitude A, offset \u03c6):\n\nA. Parent Validation\n- Check if initial_h_values is available for dimension reference\n- Store the parent's integral along with its correlation structure if improving the candidate\n\nB. Core Update Loop\nStage 1: Initial Sampling\nChoose P from [1.0, 4.0] at multiples of 0.25. For each P, generate tiling patterns based on:\n1. Symmetric checkerboard pattern\n2. Phase (\u03c6) adjustments over [-P/2, P/2]\n3. Local binary codes derived from error-correcting families\n\n- Create candidates by: candidate(x) = 1 if (round((x/\u03c6 + k*A) mod 1))%2==0 else 0, where k is integer index\n- Adjust scale to ensure \u222bcandidate=1\n\nStage 2: Optimization\nPerform gradient descent over the space:\ngrad(\u03c6) evaluators = discrete step pattern derivatives\n\nC. Repair Mechanism\nFeasibility ensured by:\nif sum(h) != n_points/2:\n  target = n_points/2\n  # Explicit balance maintenance\n  compute_error = sum(h) - target\n  while abs(compute_error) > n_points*1e-5:\n      find the effect of flipping one sample at index i\n      compute the integral impact of the flip\n      select the least disruptive correction\n\nD. Evaluation\nCompute correlation metrics using:\ncorr_shift = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\nscore = np.amax(corr_shift)/n_points   [differences by dx=2/n_points]\n\n4. Search Choices\nWe consider three variations:\nA. Fundamental pattern enforcing stride S (multiples of P)\nB. Asymmetric additive perturbations A (0.01 to 0.2 jumps)\nC. Phase derivation from equidistant points in [-P, P]\n\nDefault parameters:\nPeriod P: 3 values: 1.0, 2.0, 3.0\nAmplitude A: 0.5 fixed or logarithmic [0.1, 0.5] scale\nResolution sweep: from 100 to 500 points in batches of 50\n\n5. Budget and Robustness\nApproximate timing:\n- Optimization phase: runs concurrently across parameter sets\n- Tree operations: performed once per candidate\n- Validation checks: \u221d n\n\nFallback safeguard:\nif remaining_time<100 or current_score>worst_score:\n    return initial_h_values, compute_c5(initial_h_values), len(initial_h_values)\n\n6. Validation Contract\nMust pass these checks:\n1. sum(h) \u2248 n_points/2 (tolerance 1e-5)\n2. All values in [0,1] (verified through discrete generation)\n3. Score < current_best (if improving or time limit exceeded)\n4. Clean reevaluate using np.correlate and dx correction\n\nAny candidate with score exceeding the current_best will interrupt its computation to register the improvement.\n\nSpecial note: Optimization of dx occurs by adjusting resolution using divisor relationships of n_points and the fundamental period.",
